     "kind": "obstruction/counterexample",
     "statement": "For an independent amplitude R = sqrt(2k+1)·U^k with U uniform on (0,1) and integer k>=2, the field F = R·H has the same covariance as the centered Gaussian H but E R^2 = 1, E R^4 = (2k+1)^2/(4k+1) > 1, P(R<=s) = (s/sqrt(2k+1))^{1/k}, and E R^{-2/3} diverges. Given a caller-supplied premise E N_H([a,b]) >= p > 0, E N_F((0,t_m]) >= p/m at t_m = sqrt(2k+1)·b/m^k, so the ratio to t_m^{2/3} grows like p·m^{2k/3-1} and diverges. Equal covariance therefore cannot justify transferring the Gaussian t^{2/3} short-bar law. This is implemented as an exact-rational executable falsifier.",
